sentences.t:
Trezientas lanças son, todas tienen pendones;
seños moros mataron, todos de seños colpes;
a la tornada que fazen otros tantos [muertos] son.

sentences.l:
Three-hundred lances they-are, all have pennons;
each-one Moors they-killed, all with each-one blows;
at the turning that they-make other so-many [dead] are.

sentences.t:
Oy en este día de vos abré grand bando;
firme' son los moros, aun nos' van del campo,
a menester que los cometamos de cabo.”

sentences.l:
Today in this day from you I-shall-have great support;
firm are the Moors, still not-themselves they-go from-the field,
it-has need that them we-attack again.”

sentences.t:
los dos le fallen, y el únol ha tomado,
por la loriga ayuso la sangre destella[n]do;
bolvió la rienda por írsele del campo.

sentences.l:
the two to-him fail, and the one-to-him has taken,
through the hauberk downward the blood dripping;
he-turned the rein for to-go-himself-from-him from-the field.

sentences.t:
Arrancado es el rey Fáriz e Galve;
¡tan buen día por la cristiandad,
ca fuyen los moros della [e della] part!

sentences.l:
Routed is the king Fáriz and Galve;
so good day for the Christendom,
for flee the Moors of-that [and of-that] side!
